helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3154659				0	it	If you want to insert a formula into a text document, and you already know the correct writing, you can proceed as follows:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153818				0	it	Write the formula into your text				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3153915				0	it	Select the formula				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150213				0	it	Choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\>.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154767				0	it	Inserting a Formula using a Window				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3149875				0	it	If you want to use the $[officename] Math interface to edit a formula, choose the command \<emph\>Insert - Object - Formula\</emph\> without any text selected.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150391				0	it	The cursor waits in the Commands window and you can type the formula.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3150537				0	it	You can compose formulas using the Elements window. Open it with the menu \<emph\>View - Elements\</emph\> if it is not already open.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	par_id3155625				0	it	If the Elements window is open, use F6 to switch from the Commands window to the Elements window and back.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\keyboard.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154554				0	it	Elements window				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	tit				0	it	Changing Default Attributes				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	bm_id3145792				0	it	\<bookmark_value\>attributes; changing in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>font attributes;changing defaults\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>formatting;changing default attributes\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>defaults;changing default formatting\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>changing;default formatting\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3145792				0	it	\<variable id=\"attributes\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/attributes.xhp\" name=\"Changing Default Attributes\"\>Changing Default Attributes\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	hd_id3154484				0	it	Can default formats in $[officename] Math be modified?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3148870				0	it	Some parts of formulas are always formatted bold or italic by default.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3150210				0	it	You can remove these attributes using "nbold" and "nitalic". Example:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3143231				0	it	a + b				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3153810				0	it	nitalic a + bold b.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\attributes.xhp	0	help	par_id3149872				0	it	In the second formula, the a is not italic. The b is bold. You cannot change the plus sign by this method.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	tit				0	it	Entering Text				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	bm_id3155962				0	it	\<bookmark_value\>text strings; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>direct text; entering in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>\<bookmark_value\>inserting;text in $[officename] Math\</bookmark_value\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id5676442				0	it	\<variable id=\"text\"\>\<link href=\"text/smath/guide/text.xhp\" name=\"Entering Text\"\>Entering Text\</link\>\</variable\>				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	hd_id8509170				0	it	How to enter direct text strings that do not get interpreted?				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id337229				0	it	Some text strings get interpreted as operators automatically. Sometimes this is not what you want. If you want to write W\<emph\>*\</emph\> (a letter with a superscripted asterisk), the asterisk will be interpreted as a multiplication operator. Enclose the direct text within double quotes or add spaceholders.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_idN105D0				0	it	Examples:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id521866				0	it	An imported MathType formula contains the following string				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9413739				0	it	W rSup { size 8{*} }				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id755943				0	it	If you have set up Math to convert imported MathType formulas (in \<switchinline select=\"sys\"\>\<caseinline select=\"MAC\"\>%PRODUCTNAME - Preferences\</caseinline\>\<defaultinline\>Tools - Options\</defaultinline\>\</switchinline\> - Load/Save - Microsoft Office), you see the formula with a placeholder instead of the asterisk.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id5988952				0	it	Change {*} to {} * {} as in the following formula:				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id3304544				0	it	W rSup { size 8{} * {} }				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id4941557				0	it	You can also use W^"*" to enter the character as direct text.				20200411 15:09:53
helpcontent2	source\text\smath\guide\text.xhp	0	help	par_id9961851				0	it	Some formulas start with an = sign. Use "=" to enter that character as direct text.				20200411 15:09:53
